---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SurfaceVoxelGridBasic from "../../components/SurfaceVoxelGridBasic.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="XZ Columnar Terrain | Aaron’s Dev Experiments">
	<article class="article-page">
		<header class="article-header">
			<p class="eyebrow">
				<a href="/areas/terrain-systems">Terrain Systems</a>
			</p>

			<h1>XZ Columnar Terrain</h1>

			<p class="intro">
				An experiment in constructing a sealed voxel-owned terrain
				surface from heightmap-derived planar patches and vertical side
				fragments.
			</p>
		</header>

		<section class="article-content">
			<p>
				This experiment began with a very simple idea. I wanted to bring
				the visual world of games like <i>Terraria</i>, <i>Starbound</i
				>, and <i>RimWorld</i> into full 3D.
			</p>

			<figure class="article-figure article-figure--image">
				<img
					src="/images/rimworld.jpg"
					alt="An overhead view of a RimWorld colony with terrain, buildings, crops, and characters arranged on a tile-based world."
				/>

				<figcaption>
					<em>RimWorld</em> presents its world as a collection of discrete,
					grid-aligned units while still allowing the environment to read
					as a continuous landscape. Screenshot source:
					{" "}
					<a
						href="https://www.gamestar.de/galerien/rimworld,99361.html"
						target="_blank"
						rel="noopener noreferrer"
					>
						GameStar
					</a>.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>

			<p>
				One of the things that makes games like those unique is that
				they make their respective worlds readable as discrete units.
				You can look at the environment and immediately understand that
				it is made from units of “stuff” that can be mined, traversed,
				placed, or otherwise interacted with individually.
			</p>

			<p>
				Minecraft is the obvious 3D version of that idea. Its blocks
				make the structure of the world completely explicit. But I
				wanted to see whether I could reach beyond a block-based world
				while preserving that same sense of discrete, editable terrain.
			</p>

			<p>
				That led to a harder question:
				<i
					>how do you take a truly three-dimensional world, have it
					feel tiled and discrete, without making everything look like
					a cube?</i
				>
			</p>

			<p>
				My preliminary answer was to separate the idea of the world unit
				from the visible surface unit. I was comfortable keeping voxels
				underneath as the editable spatial structure, but I did not want
				the player to see a world made from exposed voxel faces.
				Instead, I wanted the terrain surface itself to resolve into
				deliberate, tile-like pieces.
			</p>

			<p>
				Or, to put it another way, I wanted to start with continuous 3D
				terrain and impose a readable tiled structure onto it. Each
				visible piece would still feel like an individual unit of
				terrain, while the landscape as a whole would be allowed to
				resemble a natural environment.
			</p>

			<p>
				That is where the experiment quickly became a geometry problem.
				If a continuous surface passes through a voxel grid, what should
				count as one tile—how do you construct one? The obvious choice
				is to use the individual voxels that contain the surface.
				However, the portion of the surface inside any given voxel can
				vary wildly. Some contain a broad patch of terrain that cuts
				cleanly across the voxel, while others contain only a tiny
				fragment that clips a corner or edge. I tried embracing both
				cases and worked through that for a while, but I found it
				difficult to even maintain a coherent surface, much less produce
				uniform-looking tiles. In the end, I decided that before I could
				construct tiled terrain, I needed to redefine the spatial unit
				used to create each visible surface piece.
			</p>

			<figure class="article-figure article-figure--visualization">
				<SurfaceVoxelGridBasic />

				<figcaption>
					The highlighted voxels are those intersected by the
					continuous terrain surface. Some contain broad surface
					patches, while others contain only small fragments where the
					surface clips a corner or edge. Use the controls to vary the
					surface within the voxel grid.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>

			<h2>From Voxels to Columns</h2>

			<p>The rest...</p>
		</section>

		<section class="article-project">
			<h2>Related Project</h2>

			<a class="project-card" href="/projects/plane-voxel-lab">
				<h3>Plane Voxel Lab</h3>

				<p>
					An experimental C++ / OpenGL lab for exploring voxel terrain
					representation, surface construction, rendering, and
					editable world systems.
				</p>
			</a>
		</section>
	</article>
</BaseLayout>
